{"type":"message","id":"0027dabe","parentId":"df70315e","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:27:30.153Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_B9lhaEY9IABbVGuflUpzV0tZ","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** You MUST naturally embed 2-3 markdown links inside the body text. These links MUST point to the exact source URLs provided in your RESEARCH_JSON. Example: `[according to recent data from Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/...)`\n- **CHART SOURCES:** The orchestrator will provide the charts. Simply embed exactly 2 mentions of the `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]` tag inside the H2 sections.\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** \n  - Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words)\n  - Primary keyword in first 100 words (opening hook)\n  - Primary keyword in at least one H2 and the Conclusion\n  - Secondary keywords must appear in H2 headings and their body paragraphs.\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I have the primary and secondary keywords.\n- [ ] I have the source URLs from the `RESEARCH_JSON` input.\n- [ ] I will write EXACTLY in this order: META → Hook → H2#1 → H2#2 → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n- [ ] I will write NOTHING outside this order.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nOutput this exact block at the very top. No conversational filler before it. The very first word must be \"META\".\n- SEO Title: [50–60 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: Primary Keyword + Brand Name + Value]\n- Meta Description: [150–160 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: What the article offers + Unique angle + Call to Action]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase-hyphens-only, e.g. bitcoin-price-hits-new-all-time-high]\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [e.g. \"Bitcoin price drop\"]\n- Secondary Keywords: [e.g. \"BTC resistance\", \"crypto market pullback\", \"$80K Bitcoin\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 Title — must contain primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(Do NOT add an H2 heading above these paragraphs)*\nPara 1: State the core event with a specific price or data point. 2–3 sentences. Start with the primary keyword naturally. Be direct.\nPara 2: Market reaction or supporting data. One additional observation (volume, altcoins, funding rates). 2–3 sentences.\nPara 3: One-line reframe that sets the article's tone. (\"This isn't panic. It looks like traders taking money off the table after a strong run.\")\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3 (REQUIRED): FIRST H2 SECTION\n**## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]**\n\n[1–2 intro paragraphs explaining the news at a high level.]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 1: e.g. \"Profit-Taking Shows Up at Resistance\"]**\n[1–2 short paragraphs]\n[Optional 3-item bullet list]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 2: e.g. \"Altcoins Start Losing Steam\"]**\n[2–3 very short paragraphs — 1 to 3 sentences each]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 4 (REQUIRED): SECOND H2 SECTION\n**## Key [Levels / Data Signals / Numbers] Traders Should Watch [Now / Next]**\n\n[1 intro paragraph]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 1: e.g. \"$75K Needs to Hold\"]**\n[1 paragraph — what this level means, what happens if it holds/breaks]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 2: e.g. \"$80K Remains the Barrier\"]**\n[1 paragraph]\n[Required 3-item bullet list starting with \"Look for:\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 5 (REQUIRED): CONCLUSION\n**## Conclusion**\n\nPara 1: Restate the core event in one sentence using the primary keyword. Name the key level or data point.\nPara 2: What it means for the near-term structure. One or two clean sentences.\nPara 3: Actionable close for the reader. What they should do or watch. End on a direct, confident note.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 6 (REQUIRED): FAQS\n**## FAQs**\n\n**1. Why did [primary event] happen?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**3. What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**4. How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 7 (REQUIRED): SOURCES FOOTER\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n\nSTOP GENERATING HERE. DO NOT ADD ANY BROADER CONTEXT SECTIONS. \nState your final word count on the last line of your output as: [Word Count: XXXX]\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\nBefore finalizing your generation, mentally verify:\n[ ] Exactly 1 H1, 2 H2s, 4 H3s, Conclusion, FAQs, Sources?\n[ ] No em-dashes anywhere?\n[ ] Primary keyword in H1 first 5 words?\n[ ] 2-3 anchor links embedded in body text pointing to source URLs?\n[ ] Word count stated on final line?\n[ ] No sections added beyond the 7 defined above?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779103650153}}
{"type":"message","id":"c880365f","parentId":"0027dabe","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:28:21.996Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_p9PQjdyhg6uZsviGtaNV6ePK","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-article-raw.md","content":"META\nSEO Title: Bitcoin Selloff | Coinography Breakdown for Traders\nMeta Description: See why the Bitcoin selloff pushed BTC below $77K as yields rose, ETF outflows hit, and liquidations climbed. Track the levels traders should watch.\nURL Slug: bitcoin-selloff-below-77000-bond-market-risk\nCategories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\nPrimary Keyword: Bitcoin selloff\nSecondary Keywords: Treasury yields, crypto liquidations, spot Bitcoin ETF outflows\n\n# Bitcoin selloff sends BTC below $77,000 as liquidations top $672M\n\nBitcoin selloff pressure pushed BTC below $77,000 on Sunday after a bond market rout and fresh Iran headlines hit risk assets. The move took price to roughly $76,770, a three-week low, and dragged the market back toward levels traders had defended earlier in May.\n\nThe reaction was broad. Total crypto liquidations climbed past $672 million, ether fell with Bitcoin, and the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63%, a level that tightened the macro backdrop just as demand through U.S. spot ETFs weakened.\n\nThis is not random panic. It looks like macro stress moving through rates, flows, and leverage at the same time.\n\n## Crypto liquidations: What the tape is showing\n\nThe first signal is how quickly price weakness spread once Bitcoin lost the upper-$77,000 area. [CoinDesk reported](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets) that Trump's Iran warning helped push oil higher and hit risk assets across the board, which matters because crypto had already been trading on thin confidence after a softer response to recent policy headlines.\n\nThat macro stress fed directly into positioning. [Decrypt's market report](https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off) said the selloff came with the worst weekly spot Bitcoin ETF outflows since late January, reinforcing the idea that higher yields are now one of the cleanest transmission channels into crypto price action.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### ETF outflows add to the pressure\n\nSpot Bitcoin ETF outflows matter here because they show the selloff was not driven only by perpetual swaps and retail leverage. Decrypt said the funds saw about $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, a sharp reversal from the prior week's net inflows.\n\nThat shift changes the tone of the move. When institutions are pulling exposure while Treasury yields are climbing, the market loses one of its most reliable sources of passive support. Traders then have to rely on derivatives demand and dip buyers, which is less stable during a macro risk-off session.\n\nThe message is simple. This drop was not just a chart event, it was a flows event.\n\n### Leverage made the drop faster\n\nOnce BTC slipped under nearby support, crypto liquidations accelerated the move. Longs were forced out, order books thinned, and a normal pullback turned into a sharper flush in a short window.\n\nThat is why the $77,000 zone matters more than the exact low printed on the day. The market is testing whether buyers still see this area as a reset within a broader uptrend, or whether they now view rallies as chances to reduce risk.\n\nOpen interest is also part of the setup. If price stays weak while leveraged positioning remains elevated, another wave lower becomes easier to trigger.\n\n## Key Data Signals Traders Should Watch Now in the Bitcoin selloff\n\nThe next move depends less on headlines alone and more on whether a few linked indicators stabilize together. [CoinTelegraph noted](https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) that bond-market stress and Iran-war uncertainty were both weighing on BTC, which means traders should track rates, ETF flows, and liquidation pressure as one package rather than in isolation.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### $77,000 needs to hold\n\nThis is the first near-term line that matters because it sits close to the breakdown area that turned a controlled retreat into a deeper Bitcoin selloff. If BTC can reclaim and hold above it, short-term structure can stabilize and squeeze late shorts. If it fails and sellers keep defending rebounds below that zone, traders will start looking toward the low-$70,000s as the next realistic support band.\n\n### Treasury yields remain the macro trigger\n\nTreasury yields are now doing part of the price discovery for crypto. A 10-year yield near 4.63% signals tighter financial conditions, weaker appetite for speculative exposure, and a harder path for spot Bitcoin ETF outflows to reverse quickly. If yields keep rising, crypto can stay under pressure even without a fresh negative crypto-specific headline.\n\nLook for:\n- 10-year Treasury yields to cool back from 4.63% and relieve pressure on risk assets.\n- Spot Bitcoin ETF outflows to slow after last week's $1 billion exit.\n- Crypto liquidations and open interest to reset before aggressive long positioning returns.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Bitcoin selloff pushed BTC below $77,000 as rising Treasury yields, geopolitical stress, and heavy liquidations hit the market at the same time. That combination is why this level matters more than a single intraday wick.\n\nNear-term structure now depends on whether price can reclaim breakdown levels while macro pressure eases. If yields stay high and ETF flows stay negative, rebounds may remain fragile.\n\nTraders should watch $77,000 first, then monitor rates and ETF data for confirmation. If those signals improve together, the selloff can cool quickly. If they do not, defense comes before conviction.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did Bitcoin slide below $77,000 happen?**\n\nBitcoin fell as macro pressure intensified across markets. Rising Treasury yields tightened financial conditions, Trump's Iran warning lifted geopolitical risk, and leveraged crypto positions were forced out as support broke. The move then fed on itself through liquidations. That is why the drop looked fast and broad rather than isolated to one exchange or one headline.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger correction?**\n\nIt could become one, but the answer depends on follow-through. If Bitcoin quickly reclaims $77,000 and liquidations cool, this can still look like a sharp reset after a strong run. If yields stay elevated and ETF outflows continue, the market may treat every bounce as a selling opportunity. That would increase the odds of a deeper correction toward lower support zones.\n\n**3. What levels and signals should traders watch now?**\n\nThe first level is $77,000 because that area now separates recovery from continued weakness. Beyond price, traders should watch the 10-year Treasury yield, spot ETF flow data, and futures open interest. A healthier setup would include lower yields, smaller outflows, and less crowded leverage. Without that combination, BTC can remain vulnerable to another flush.\n\n**4. How does this affect the rest of the crypto market?**\n\nAltcoins usually struggle when Bitcoin is falling for macro reasons instead of idiosyncratic crypto news. Ether already moved lower alongside BTC, and broad liquidations show that traders were cutting risk across the board. If Bitcoin stabilizes, majors can recover with it. If BTC keeps slipping, lower-liquidity tokens can underperform and see larger percentage moves.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n\nThe size of the ETF outflow stands out because Decrypt said it was the largest weekly exit since late January. That links this move to a familiar pattern where rates and macro shocks weaken institutional demand first, then price follows. Bitcoin has absorbed similar liquidations before, but the path usually depends on whether external conditions calm down quickly. In this case, the bond market is the key variable.\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n\nThe main risk is assuming the first bounce is the bottom before macro pressure actually eases. Elevated yields, weaker ETF demand, and still-heavy derivatives positioning can create another leg lower if support fails again. Volatility can also stay high while traders react to bond-market moves and geopolitical headlines. In the short term, confirmation matters more than hope.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n\n[Word Count: 1188]"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1594,"output":3778,"cacheRead":8192,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":13564,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779103650153,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgqQAO3gNGd1CWf0VPAMoTbFC9gVn"}}
